# musing-ai-service (`service/`)

The optional AI second-brain layer: semantic search, summarization, related pages, over
musing's notes. Own package (`service/package.json`), own toolchain — **not** part of the
root npm workspace. Deployed independently to Cloud Run; the frontend calls it **cross-origin
directly** (no same-origin proxy — see `AGENTS.md` and the "Deploy musing-ai-service" README
section for why).

## Must preserve

- **Every required env var fails fast at startup**, not silently defaults (`lib/env.ts`'s
  `requireEnv`/`requireEnvNumber`, used throughout). Follow this pattern for any new required
  config — never let a missing var degrade into wrong-but-running behavior.
- **Two separately-billed LLM providers**: Anthropic (`lib/anthropic.ts`, official SDK) for
  chat/summarization, Voyage AI (`lib/voyage.ts`, raw `fetch` — no official SDK exists, don't
  add one without verified docs) for embeddings. `ai_usage` and the budget middleware
  (`middleware/budget.ts`) track and cap each separately.
- **`requireAuth` (`middleware/auth.ts`) verifies the caller's Supabase JWT** via the
  service-role client (`lib/supabaseAdmin.ts`) — this is the real access boundary. Cloud Run
  itself is deployed `--allow-unauthenticated`; don't add GCP-IAM-based access control here,
  callers are end-user browsers with no GCP identity.
- **`requireBudget(provider)` gates every route that calls a paid provider** — including
  `/api/embed-page` (Voyage), not just the LLM-calling routes the original design doc implied.
  Add it to any new paid-provider route.
- **`llmRateLimiter` (`lib/tokenBucket.ts`)**: `await .acquire()` before every outbound
  Anthropic/Voyage call, process-wide, independent of per-user budgets.
- **CORS (`lib/cors.ts`)**: `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` is a plain (non-secret) comma-separated repo
  env var/secret, not Secret Manager — deliberate, since it's not sensitive. If it ever needs
  a value containing a comma in `deploy-service.yml`, the comma must be escaped there (the
  `deploy-cloudrun` action's `env_vars` parses raw commas as entry separators) — see that
  workflow's `escape_origins` step.
- **Two Postgres RPC functions** (`match_note_embeddings`, `match_related_pages` in
  `supabase/schema.sql`) back `/api/search` and `/api/related-pages` — pgvector similarity
  search isn't expressible through the Supabase JS query builder. Both are revoked from
  `anon`/`authenticated` and granted only to `service_role`, since they take `match_user_id`
  as a plain parameter instead of reading `auth.uid()`. Never weaken that grant.

## Local dev

```bash
cd service
npm install
npm run dev   # tsx watch, reads service/.env.local (see service/.env.example)
```

Root `npm run lint`/`format:check`/`build`/`test` do **not** cover `service/` — it has its
own scripts, its own `vitest.config.ts` (needed so it doesn't inherit the root's FE-only
`setupFiles`), and its own CI steps in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (`working-directory:
service`). Run `npm run lint && npm run test:run && npm run build && npm run format:check`
inside `service/` when touching it.

## Deploy

`.github/workflows/deploy-service.yml` deploys on push to `main` touching `service/**`.
Skips (doesn't fail) until the GCP repo secrets/Secret Manager entries exist — see the
"Deploy musing-ai-service" section of the root `README.md` for the full setup, including the
IAM roles actually required (discovered live during the first real deploy: Cloud Build
Editor, Artifact Registry Administrator, Storage Admin, on top of the obvious Cloud Run
Admin/Service Account User/Secret Manager Secret Accessor).

## Tests

Vitest, colocated `*.test.ts` next to the module under test — same convention as `src/`, but
configured separately (`service/vitest.config.ts`). Only pure logic is unit-tested here
(`chunking`, `clampLimit`, `tokenBucket`) — no route/handler-level tests yet.
